%I A400946 #24 Oct 25 2026 22:14:01
%S A400946 1,8,25,9,4,3,13,2,12,32,61,33,24,19,5,18,41,72,113,73,60,51,85,50,84,
%T A400946 128,181,129,112,99,145,98,144,200,265,201,180,163,221,162,220,288,
%U A400946 365,289,264,243,313,242,312,392,481,393,364,339,421,338,420,512,613,513,480,451,545,450
%N A400946 Squares visited by knight moves on a diagonally numbered Q1 board and always taking the available unvisited square with the shortest distance to the bisecting line of Q1. In the case of a tie, first squares with the smaller row index are preferred, second the square with larger column index is preferred. Starting square is labeled 1.
%C A400946 Board is numbered as follows:
%C A400946   1 2 4 7 11 16 ...
%C A400946   3 5 8 12 17 ...
%C A400946   6 9 13 18 ...
%C A400946   10 14 19 ...
%C A400946   15 20 ...
%C A400946   21 ...
%C A400946   ...
%C A400946 The knight starts at position (1,1). The knight moves in the open field spanned by the positive x- and y-axis, i.e., the first quadrant Q1.
%C A400946 In case the knight has a choice between squares, which have equal distance to the bisecting line, first the square with its row index being smaller than the row indices of the alternative squares is preferred. If this first tiebreaking rule still leaves an optional square, the square with its column index being larger than the column index of the alternative square is chosen to break the tie.
%H A400946 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400946/b400946.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..5000</a>
%H A400946 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400946/a400946.svg">Illustration of the path</a>
%H A400946 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400946/a400946.py.txt">Python program</a>
%F A400946 a(1)=1, a(2)=8, a(3)=25, a(4)=9, a(5)=4, a(6)=3, a(7)=13, a(8)=2, a(9)=12, a(10)=32, a(11)=61, a(12)=33, a(13)=24, a(14)=19, a(15)=5, a(16)=18, a(17)=41 and for n>=18
%F A400946 a(n)=c(n+6)*b1(n)+c(n+5)*b2(n)+c(n+4)*b3(n)+c(n+3)*b4(n)+c(n+2)*b5(n)+c(n+1)*b6(n)+c(n)*b7(n)+c(n-1)*b8(n) where
%F A400946   b1(n) = 8*((n-10)/8)^2+32*(n-10)/8+32,
%F A400946   b2(n) = 8*((n-11)/8)^2+44*(n-11)/8+61,
%F A400946   b3(n) = 8*((n-12)/8)^2+32*(n-12)/8+33,
%F A400946   b4(n) = 8*((n-13)/8)^2+28*(n-13)/8+24,
%F A400946   b5(n) = 8*((n-14)/8)^2+24*(n-14)/8+19,
%F A400946   b6(n) = 8*((n-15)/8)^2+36*(n-15)/8+41,
%F A400946   b7(n) = 8*((n-16)/8)^2+24*(n-16)/8+18,
%F A400946   b8(n) = 8*((n-17)/8)^2+36*(n-17)/8+40,
%F A400946 and c(n) = A253513(n).
%F A400946 G.f.: x * (1 + 7*x + 17*x^2 - 16*x^3 - 5*x^4 - x^5 + 10*x^6 - 11*x^7 + 8*x^8 + 6*x^9 - 5*x^10 + 4*x^11 + x^12 - 3*x^13 - 34*x^14 + 35*x^15 + 4*x^16 - 2*x^17 + 72*x^22 - 72*x^23 - 2*x^24 + 2*x^25 - 36*x^30 + 36*x^31 + x^32 - x^33) / ((1+x)^2 * (1+x^2)^2 * (1+x^4)^2 * (1-x)^3). - _Sean A. Irvine_, Oct 25 2026
%e A400946 Board after 30 steps: (0's are unvisited squares).
