%e A400946   ...
%e A400946 a(1) = 1 because 1 is at position (1,1) which is numbered 1.
%e A400946 a(2) = 8 because 2 is at position (2,3) which is numbered 8. In this case, the tiebreaking rule chooses (2,3) over the other available candidate (3,2), because both positions have equal distance to the bisecting line, but (2,3) exhibits the smaller row index.
%e A400946 a(3) = 25 because 3 is at position (4,4) which is numbered 25.
%e A400946 a(4) = 9 because 4 is at position (3,2) which is numbered 9. In this case, the tiebreaking rules chooses (3,2) over the other available candidates (5,6) and (6,5), because all three options have equal distance to the bisecting line, but (3,2) exhibits the smaller row index.
%e A400946 ...
%e A400946 a(10) = 32 because 10 is at position (4,5) which is numbered 32. In this case, the subordinate tiebreaking rule choses (4,5) over the other available candidate (4,3), because both have equal distance to the bisecting line and both have a row index 4, but (4,5) exhibits the larger column index.
%Y A400946 Cf. A001844, A204459, A204467, A316588, A316667, A326916, A326922, A326924, A328908, A328909, A399236, A399929, A399930, A400771, A400937.
